meta {
  name: A filter cannot widen
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/student-documents?student_id=44444444-4444-4444-8444-444444444444
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.count: eq 0
  res.body.student_documents: length 0
  res.body.page.limit: eq 50
  res.body.page.offset: eq 0
  res.body.page.has_more: eq false
}

docs {
  Naming a pupil in student_id does not ask the API to fetch that pupil. The
  filter is ANDed onto a query that has already been narrowed to who the
  caller may see, so the most a filter can ever do is subtract. Here it
  subtracts everything and the answer is an empty list.

  200 with nothing in it, not 400 and not 403. The three cases -- no such
  pupil, a pupil with no documents, and a pupil this caller has no claim to --
  are deliberately indistinguishable from the outside. Telling them apart is
  what would turn this parameter into a way of probing the roll.

  What this request does NOT test. The collection runs as one unrestricted
  caller, and for such a caller the narrowing predicate is a no-op: there is
  nothing underneath the filter to AND onto. So the empty list above is the
  filter subtracting from everything, and it proves the filter cannot widen.
  It says nothing whatever about whether the scoping works, because no scoping
  was in play. A green run here is not evidence that a guardian is confined to
  their own children.

  Testing that needs a caller who is actually narrowed -- a real CAS token for
  a guardian, a class teacher, a subject teacher -- which this collection
  cannot mint. api/README.md says the same thing about the run as a whole. The
  narrowing itself is covered in Go, in
  internal/transport/http/documentscope_test.go and guardianacademic_test.go,
  and that is where to look for the proof this request cannot give.
}
